<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">9 January in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">12 events that happened on 9 January, from 1127 to 1957.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaifeng.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1127 CE <span class="tih-ago">899 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Jin armies sack Kaifeng, capital of the Northern Song</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingkang-incident">Jingkang incident</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> troops of the Jin dynasty sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> and carried off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> emperors Qinzong and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-huizong">Huizong</a>. The Song court fled south, where it ruled as the Southern Song.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingkang-incident">Jingkang incident</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1431 CE <span class="tih-ago">595 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Trial of Joan of Arc opens in Rouen</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A church court led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Pierre Cauchon, an ally of the English, began proceedings against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> on charges of heresy. She was condemned and burned at the stake on 30 May.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1693 CE <span class="tih-ago">333 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">First shock of the great Sicilian earthquake strikes</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A strong foreshock damaged towns across south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Two days later the main earthquake devastated the region, killing some 60,000 people and leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> rebuilding of Noto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catania</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1788 CE <span class="tih-ago">238 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Connecticut ratifies the US Constitution</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>'s convention approved the Constitution by 128 votes to 40, making it the fifth state to ratify. Nine states were needed for the new frame of government to take effect.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1792 CE <span class="tih-ago">234 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Treaty of Jassy ends the Russo-Turkish War</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and ceded the coast between the Bug and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> rivers. The treaty confirmed Russia as the dominant power on the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1793 CE <span class="tih-ago">233 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Blanchard makes the first balloon flight in North America</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Watched by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>, the French aeronaut Jean-Pierre Blanchard ascended from a prison yard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>. He landed some 46 minutes later in Deptford, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1799 CE <span class="tih-ago">227 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pitt's income tax becomes law in Britain</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger">William Pitt the Younger</a>'s income tax, set at two shillings in the pound on higher incomes, received royal assent to fund the war against Revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Abolished in 1816, it returned for good in 1842.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1806 CE <span class="tih-ago">220 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Admiral Nelson is buried in St Paul's Cathedral</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After a vast procession through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Horatio Nelson</a> was laid to rest in the crypt of St Paul's. He had been killed at his victory at Trafalgar the previous October.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1861 CE <span class="tih-ago">165 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Cadets fire on the Star of the West in Charleston Harbor</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Southern guns fired on the merchant ship Star of the West as it tried to resupply Fort Sumter, forcing it to turn back. The shots are sometimes called the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1916 CE <span class="tih-ago">110 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Last Allied troops evacuate Gallipoli</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">British troops left Cape Helles, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli-campaign">Gallipoli campaign</a> in an Ottoman victory. The evacuation was the most successful part of an eight-month operation that cost well over 100,000 lives on both sides.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1923 CE <span class="tih-ago">103 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Juan de la Cierva's autogiro makes its first successful flight</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At Getafe near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, the Spanish engineer's C.4 autogiro flew using a freely turning rotor for lift. The design pioneered rotor technology later used in helicopters.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1957 CE <span class="tih-ago">69 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Anthony Eden resigns as British Prime Minister</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Citing ill health, Eden stepped down two months after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had been forced by American pressure to withdraw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-macmillan">Harold Macmillan</a> succeeded him.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-macmillan">Harold Macmillan</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-eden">Anthony Eden</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a></div></div></li></ol>
